fact WorkItem(id: "WI-783", description: some(value: "NAMED ARGUMENTS in a function-VALUE application are bound by WRITTEN ORDER, not by name -- a silently wrong answer with no trap and no diagnostic. MEASURED end-to-end (verified directly, not inferred): 'operation sub2(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 = a - b' + 'operation apply2(f: (acc: Int64, x: Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(x: 10, acc: 3)' + 'operation drive() -> Int64 = apply2(sub2)' loads clean and evaluates to 7 (i.e. 10 - 3: the labels are ignored and the operands bind positionally in the order WRITTEN); changing ONLY the argument order at the call site to 'f(acc: 3, x: 10)' evaluates to -7. The declared binder names acc/x are inert. This is strictly worse than the WI-775 failure mode it neighbours: WI-775 traps loudly at eval, this returns a plausible wrong number. Distinct from WI-775 (closed: DATA-tuple key bridging) and from WI-782 (parameter-list ORDER/ARITY/slot-collapse in the TYPE relation) -- this one is in EVALUATION of an application whose callee is a function value, and it reproduces with MATCHING keys on both sides, so no positional bridging is involved. A control shows the name-keyed path is not merely mis-bound but unimplemented for closures: a 2-binder 'lambda (acc, x) -> ...' applied as 'f(x: 10, acc: 3)' traps ArityMismatch{op: closure, expected: 1, got: 2}. ACCEPTANCE: either bind named arguments of a function-value application to the arrow's declared binder names (and raise on an unknown label), or REJECT named arguments in that position with a located diagnostic -- silently reordering is not an option; regression test drives both orderings end-to-end and asserts they agree; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T20:11:23Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-783", author: "user", content: "The permuted-arrow regression recorded against this ticket has a TWIN, filed as WI-788: a destructuring binder over an out-of-order tuple LITERAL binds by source position while the typer typed the binders by the type's declared field order, so `f((b: \\\"ess\\\", a: 3))` against `(a: Int64, b: String)` loads clean and returns a String from an `-> Int64` operation. Same root cause as this ticket's own regression -- `align_named_tuple_fields`' by-name rung admitting a permutation the runtime does not perform -- one on the arrow-parameter-list side (here) and one on the data-tuple side (WI-788). Both gate on WI-782, which has been annotated accordingly. Recording the link so neither is fixed in isolation: they are the same rung of the same function, and a fix to one that does not generalise will leave the other live.", at: "2026-07-20T08:26:26Z")
